{{also|Bree|breé|breë}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/bɹiː/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|iː|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|brewe}}, {{m|enm|bre}}, {{m|enm|bregh}}, from {{inh|en|ang|brēġ||eyelid}} {{i|Anglian dialect}}. Compare West Saxon {{m|ang|brǣw}}, {{m|ang|brēaw}}, {{m|ang|brēaġ||eyelid}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*brēwō}}. Cognate with {{cog|nl|wenkbrauw|(wenk)brauw}}, {{cog|de|Braue}}. Apparently related to {{m|en|brow}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|or|dialectal|Scotland}} The [[eyelid]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|or|dialectal|Scotland}} The [[eyebrow]].
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} The [[brow]]; [[forehead]].

===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|bre}}, {{m|enm|breie||broth; gravy}}, apparently from {{inh|en|ang|brīw}}, {{m|ang|brīġ||pottage; porridge}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*brīwaz||porridge; mash}}, whence also {{cog|de|Brei}}, {{cog|nl|brij}}. Alternatively, the word could be a cognate of {{cog|de|Brühe||broth}}, from {{cog|gmh|brüeje}}, from the verb {{m|gmh|brüejen||to scald, boil}}, from {{cog|gem-pro|*brōaną}}, whence modern {{cog|de|brühen}}, {{cog|nl|broeien}}, {{cog|gml|brȫjen}}. This is less likely, however, since the verb is not attested in English nor in {{cog|non|-}}. Both paths eventually lead to the {{der|en|ine-pro|-}} root {{m|ine-pro|*bʰrewh₁-||to boil, toss, cook, brew}}, whence also {{cog|en|broth}} and {{m|en|brew}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|outside|Scotland}} [[broth|Broth]].

